  +-- CONTRADICTION -------> ALTERNATIVE PATH
  |
  +-- VALID DERIVATION ----> VERIFICATION
  |
  +-- BUDGET EXHAUSTED ---> PARTIAL RESULT

The reasoner may issue multiple
knowledge queries during one task.

Knowledge retrieval is not a
single preprocessing stage.

The reasoner operates without
a permanently resident LLM.

Failure to find a proof does not
establish impossibility.


==================================================
9. KNOWLEDGE QUERY INTERFACE
==================================================

The reasoning engine requests
relevant knowledge on demand.

QUERY FIELDS:

- Current goal
- Known premises
- Required concepts
- Constraints
- Search depth
- Maximum results
- Retrieval budget

RESULT FIELDS:

- Matching knowledge objects
- Relevant subgraph
- Applicable derivations
- Contradictions
- Missing dependencies
- Retrieval completeness status

EXAMPLE INTERFACE:

struct KnowledgeQuery {
    GoalId goal;
    vector<ObjectId> knownPremises;
    vector<Constraint> constraints;
    size_t maxResults;
};

struct KnowledgeResult {
    vector<KnowledgeObject> objects;
    vector<Derivation> derivations;
    bool complete;
};

KnowledgeResult query(
    const KnowledgeQuery& request
);

A partial result may occur because of:

- Missing IPFS blocks
- Network timeouts
- Search limits
- Unavailable archives
- Memory limits

Incomplete retrieval must not be
interpreted as absence of knowledge.


==================================================
10. CHAIN OF KNOWLEDGE (CoK)
==================================================

The CoK is a persistent directed
hypergraph represented through
a structured knowledge DSL.

OBJECT TYPES:

- CLAIM
- FACT
- RULE
- ASSUMPTION
- HYPOTHESIS
- DERIVATION
- EVIDENCE
- GOAL
- ACTION
- EXPERIMENT
- TEST_RESULT
- CONTRADICTION

RELATIONSHIP TYPES:

- REQUIRES
- IMPLIES
- SUPPORTS
- CONTRADICTS
- DEPENDS_ON
- PRODUCES
- TESTS
- ALTERNATIVE_TO

DERIVATION STRUCTURE:

TYPE: DERIVATION
ID: content_hash
CONCLUSION: claim_id
PREMISES: [claim_id, claim_id]
RULE: rule_id
ASSUMPTIONS: [...]
EVIDENCE: [...]
SOURCE_REFERENCES: [...]
VERIFICATION: status
SCHEMA_VERSION: version

KNOWLEDGE STATES:

PROVISIONAL
VERIFIED
DISPUTED
INVALIDATED
UNRESOLVED
ARCHIVED

Every derivation preserves:

- Premises
- Applied inference rule
- Assumptions
- Supporting evidence
- Source provenance
- Verification status
- Dependency references

Formal logical validity and
empirical truth are tracked
independently.

Contradictions and invalidated
derivations remain traceable.


==================================================
11. KNOWLEDGE DSL
==================================================

GammaThink uses a deterministic,
machine-parsable Domain-Specific
Language as its canonical logical
knowledge representation.

The knowledge base is not primarily
stored as natural-language paragraphs.

It contains structured objects
describing claims, entities, rules,
relationships, evidence and derivations.

The DSL is shared between:

- Neural language interpreter
- Knowledge compiler
- Symbolic reasoning engine
- Verification engine
- Snapshot compiler
- Human graph visualiser

The DSL is independent of physical
storage.

GKS snapshots may store a compact
binary encoding of the same structure.

Human-readable DSL can be reconstructed
from the binary representation.


==================================================
12. EXAMPLE KNOWLEDGE DSL
==================================================

CLAIM C001 {
    SUBJECT: "water"
    PROPERTY: "freezing_point"
    VALUE: 0
    UNIT: "celsius"
    CONDITIONS: {
        pressure: "1_atm"
        substance: "pure_water"
    }
    STATUS: VERIFIED
    EVIDENCE: [E001]
}

CLAIM C002 {
    SUBJECT: "sample_A"
    PROPERTY: "temperature"
    VALUE: -5
    UNIT: "celsius"
    STATUS: VERIFIED
    EVIDENCE: [E002]
}

RULE R001 {
    NAME: "freezing_condition"
    PREMISES: [C001, C002]
    ADDITIONAL_CONDITIONS: [
        "sample_A_is_pure_water",
        "sample_A_pressure_is_1_atm",
        "equilibrium_conditions_apply"
    ]
    CONCLUSION: C003
}

CLAIM C003 {
    SUBJECT: "sample_A"
    PROPERTY: "equilibrium_phase"
    VALUE: "solid"
    STATUS: PROVISIONAL
}

DERIVATION D001 {
    PREMISES: [C001, C002]
    RULE: R001
    CONCLUSION: C003
    ASSUMPTIONS: [
        "sample_A_is_pure_water",
        "sample_A_pressure_is_1_atm",
        "equilibrium_conditions_apply"
    ]
    VERIFICATION: PENDING
}

This is illustrative DSL syntax.

The final implementation requires
a versioned grammar and parser.

Empirical claims must distinguish
measurements, assumptions and
conditional rules.


==================================================
13. HYPERGRAPH REPRESENTATION
==================================================

The parsed DSL forms a directed,
typed knowledge hypergraph.

A hypergraph supports relationships
involving multiple premises.

EXAMPLE:

       +-----------+       +-----------+
       | CLAIM C001|       | CLAIM C002|
       +-----+-----+       +-----+-----+
             |                   |
             +---------+---------+
                       |
                       v
                +-------------+
                | RULE R001   |
                | DERIVATION  |
                | D001        |
                +------+------+
                       |
                       v
                +-------------+
                | CLAIM C003  |
                +-------------+

The derivation requires the
combined premises and applicable
conditions.

It is not equivalent to independent
binary implications.

SUPPORTED STRUCTURES:

- Multi-premise inference
- Alternative derivations
- Shared dependencies
- Cross-domain relationships
- Contradictory hypotheses
- Evidence-to-claim relationships
- Provenance tracing
- Recursive dependency traversal

Each derivation is represented by
an explicit relationship object
or hyperedge.


